 range of brilliant technological achievements, for example, the double-acting piston-bellows, the rotary winnowing-fan, and the water-powered metallurgical blowing-machine (ancestor of the steam-engine); as well as for some extraordinary insights and predictions in aeronautics.\n\n### Other editions - View all\n\n | Science and Civilisation in China, Volume 5, Part 12\nJoseph Needham,Rose Kerr,Nigel Wood\nLimited preview - 2004\n\n### Bibliographic information\n\nTitle  | Science and Civilisation in China: Volume 4, Physics and Physical Technology, Part 2, Mechanical Engineering\nScience and Civilisation in China\nAuthor  | Joseph Needham\nEdition  | reprint\nPublisher  | Cambridge University Press, 1965\nISBN  | 0521058031, 9780521058032\nLength  | 816 pages\nSubjects  | Technology & Engineering › History\nScience / History\nTechnology & Engineering / History\n   |  \nExport Citation  | BiBTeX EndNote RefMan--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nScience and Civilisation in China, Part 2, Mechanical Engineering - Joseph Needham - Google Books (https://books.google.com/books/about/Science_and_Civilisation_in_China_Part_2.html?id=SeGyrCfYs2AC)\nciteturn163search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 61.8 years ago; Crawled: last month; \n\n# Science and Civilisation in China, Part 2, Mechanical Engineering\n\nJoseph NeedhamCambridge University Press, Jan 2, 1965 - History - 816 pages As Dr Needham's immense undertaking gathers momentum it has been found necessary to subdivide volumes into parts, each to be bound and published separately. The first part of Volume 4, already published, deals with the physical sciences; the second with the diverse applications of physics in the many branches of mechanical engineering; and the third will deal with civil and hydraulic engineering and nautical technology. With this part of Volume 4, then, we come to the application by the Chinese of physical principles in the control of forces and in the 